some of the most exciting and valuable  entrepreneurial opportunities are based  on innovations that combine ideas from  existing product categories what I'm  interested in is trying to understand  what are the advantages of being an  established firm from one of those  established product categories in those  kinds of innovations versus the  advantages of being a startup consumers  and buyers bring to the market they're  established understandings of existing  products and those understandings serve  as lenses through which they interpret  new products it is the way in which they  make sense of those new products so on  the one hand in many settings  established firms might have an  advantage because their association with  an established product makes it easier  for consumers to understand what the new  product and the innovation does  another settings startups might have an  advantage in part that's because they  don't have an existing identity to  create confusion because sometimes what  happens is that the new product is  sufficiently different from the existing  products that if you try to interpret it  through the existing product lenses  you're gonna misunderstand and you're  not gonna see the potential of the  product when established firms are  trying to move into a new market so for  example they come up with a new  innovation in their labs they face a  challenge which is consumers already  associate us with our existing products  and so how do we now think about getting  them to understand the product that  we're offering given that they're going  to understand the product that we're  offering through the lens or through the  category that they already associate  with us as a producer so if I'm like on  and I produce a digital camera people  are going to understand the digital  camera in terms of their understanding  of me as a producer of film cameras so  what they're going to bring to bear on  that is how does a film camera work what  does it do for me and is that a virtue  or a liability in terms of their ability  to understand my product as a digital  camera if I'm night on it's probably an  advantage and in fact that's what my  research shows it is an advantage but if  I am HP it's actually a disadvantage  right because when you on HP you look at  me and you think computer and if you  think of computer well there's a digital  camera really do what a computer does  one of the mistakes I think that a lot  of entrepreneurial firms make in that  kind of situation is they make appeals  to a lot of different product categories  at the same time so they say our product  is a little bit like this and it's a  little bit like that and it's a little  bit like that it combines the best  elements of all these different kinds of  things together what the research shows  though is that when you combine lots of  different elements from lots of  different categories together what it  creates in the mind of the buyer or the  consumer is confusion and when they get  confused they ignore you and so it can  be a problem for an entrepreneur to have  this kind of confused identity when in  fact it could be an advantage to have a  much more crisp and clear identity going  into the market the problem with that of  course is that you don't know ahead of  time necessarily which identity is going  to be the best so this is an important  element of an entrepreneurial strategy  is you have to make a bet on which  identity do you think is going to be the  one that's going to be best able to help  consumers make sense of the product that  you're bringing to market
 

